WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.010
Davao, in the Philippines.

2
00:00:03.130 --> 00:00:06.360
Sixty-four farmers grow
cacao for one co-op.

3
00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:10.140
Every delivery is weighed,
and written on a paper slip.

4
00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:15.900
Then a chocolate maker overseas pays
one premium, for the whole harvest.

5
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:18.500
One payment.

6
00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:20.590
Sixty-four farmers.

7
00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:24.940
The split is done by hand,
from a box of slips.

8
00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:27.850
A carbon copy gets counted twice.

9
00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.240
A fine appears that no rule allows.

10
00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:33.880
Farmers can't check their share.

11
00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:37.190
The buyer can't prove the money arrived.

12
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:42.610
Fair Split turns a season of
paper into pay anyone can check.

13
00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:47.850
The vision: every co-op,
every farmer, paid fairly,

14
00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:51.330
by PayPal or in cash,
with proof for the buyer.

15
00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:53.440
Here's what works today.

16
00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:56.360
The buyer pays one PayPal invoice.

17
00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.970
Claude reads every slip, twice.

18
00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:02.410
If the readings disagree,
a person decides.

19
00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:09.000
Code, not AI, works out every share, to
the cent, and stops what it can't explain.

20
00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:13.040
This slip was counted twice:
once from its pink copy.

21
00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:17.960
Once fixed, almost eighteen dollars
goes back to sixty-three farmers.

22
00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:20.760
Two officers sign the same batch:

23
00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:24.080
the treasurer at her desk,
the chair on his phone.

24
00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:26.850
PayPal pays the farmers
who have an account.

25
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:29.790
The rest get cash, on the same statement.

26
00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:33.830
Nang Celing reads her pay in
her own language, or listens.

27
00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:37.380
One tap shows the slip behind each peso.

28
00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:41.090
And the buyer checks every
line, in her own browser.

29
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.500
Before: a box of slips, and trust.

30
00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:49.450
Now: one pay run that explains itself.

31
00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:51.800
In our demo, under a minute.

32
00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.247
Under the hood, it starts
simple: paper in, pay out.

33
00:01:56.637 --> 00:01:58.397
Four stages sit between.

34
00:01:59.267 --> 00:02:00.856
First, AI reads.

35
00:02:01.387 --> 00:02:04.846
Claude reads each slip twice,
and code compares the readings.

36
00:02:05.661 --> 00:02:09.755
Second, code computes:
every share in whole cents,

37
00:02:09.942 --> 00:02:12.662
under a rate card hashed
before the season.

38
00:02:13.382 --> 00:02:14.961
Third, people approve.

39
00:02:15.573 --> 00:02:18.483
Two officers sign one
fingerprint of the batch,

40
00:02:18.634 --> 00:02:20.914
and the server recomputes
it before paying.

41
00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:24.007
One changed cent voids both signatures.

42
00:02:24.957 --> 00:02:30.363
Fourth, PayPal pays: the invoice through
PayPal's Agent Toolkit, payouts through

43
00:02:30.403 --> 00:02:33.121
our own typed tool, saved first,

44
00:02:33.408 --> 00:02:37.039
sent exactly once,
confirmed by signed webhooks.

45
00:02:37.689 --> 00:02:40.850
Underneath, every step is
chained into one ledger.

46
00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:48.630
Fair Split is a hackathon MVP:

47
00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:52.450
PayPal runs on our simulator,
and the co-op is synthetic.

48
00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:55.900
The vision is real. Fair Split.

49
00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:57.970
Every peso, traced.
